WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.176
The same model as a neural network.

2
00:00:02.456 --> 00:00:09.795
The second half of the video rebuilds the bigram model with gradient descent, and this is where it starts looking like deep learning.

3
00:00:10.075 --> 00:00:17.520
Encode the current character as a one-hot vector x in R^27 (all zeros except a 1 at its index).

4
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:24.541
Multiply by a 27 by 27 weight matrix W to get 27 numbers called logits.

5
00:00:24.821 --> 00:00:31.157
Exponentiate and normalize (the softmax ) to get a probability distribution over the next character.

6
00:00:31.437 --> 00:00:33.677
The loss is the same average NLL.

7
00:00:33.957 --> 00:00:35.877
Backpropagate and step W.

8
00:00:36.157 --> 00:00:38.824
Two notes in the video tie this back to counting.

9
00:00:39.104 --> 00:00:46.528
First (01:47:49), x times W with a one-hot x just selects row i of W.

10
00:00:46.808 --> 00:00:59.331
So the network is a lookup table of logits, one row per current character, and an embedding layer in a transformer is the same idea: indexing a matrix instead of multiplying by a one-hot vector.

11
00:00:59.611 --> 00:01:03.685
Second, the softmax of row i is the model’s P(next given i).

12
00:01:03.965 --> 00:01:13.992
The NLL is minimized when that softmax equals the empirical row distribution, which happens when the logits equal log counts plus any per-row constant.

13
00:01:14.272 --> 00:01:20.544
So gradient descent converges toward the counting model: exp of the logits plays the role of the counts.

14
00:01:20.824 --> 00:01:36.419
The second note (01:50:18) maps smoothing onto the network: adding a penalty proportional to the mean of W squared pulls all logits toward 0, which pushes every row toward uniform, exactly what pseudo-counts do.

15
00:01:36.699 --> 00:01:41.371
Regularization in neural networks is, very often, a prior in disguise.
